From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 03:09:12 -0400
Subject: feat: dark pages, apps from pages, local tabs, the session mark, and
 the round of 2026-10-18

The owner's evening round of 2026-10-18: four features, the defects the
installed-build runs of S-173 found, and the open defects, in one patch
because they touch the same shared files (the command bar, the accelerator
tables, the preferences, Settings' Stedding page, the tab row):

- Dark pages (docs/features/dark-pages.md): one switch, a moon button at the
  address row's right end, Option-Shift-Cmd-D (Ctrl+Alt+Shift+D), a command
  bar row, a Mac View menu item and a Settings section, turns every website
  dark with Chromium's own automatic dark mode, applied by Blink at paint time
  from the first frame; per-site exceptions in both directions through the
  AUTO_DARK_WEB_CONTENT content setting, now registered on desktop; open pages
  repaint in place; the browser's own pages, DevTools and extension pages are
  never darkened; the address row and an app's title bar take a darkened
  theme colour.
- Apps from pages (docs/features/web-apps.md): Add to Dock on the Mac, as
  Safari has it, and Install page as app on Windows, through Chromium's web
  app machinery; the app keeps the session of the Space it was made in, and
  on the Mac its Dock tile comes and goes with it.
- Local development tabs (docs/features/local-tabs.md): a tab on this machine's
  own server wears a dashed yellow and black tape round its row while it is the
  active one, and shows its host and port under the title.
- The session mark (docs/features/sessions.md S32 to S34, spaces.md B38): a
  Space with an independent session is marked on its chip, the title row, the
  menus, Settings and the command bar; pinning a tab out of such a Space says
  so, with Undo.
- The sidebar's quiet controls (sidebar Y27): the Archived row and the Clear
  line sit back in a softer shade until hovered, pressed or focused.
- Windows: the network service runs in its own sandbox (windows N15).
- The essentials card is a frosted tile with no edge, as Arc draws its
  favourites (tabs R32); its 1 DIP edge was Chromium's outline, whose
  Material colour took the Space's hue.
- Defects: a restored row lost its page's title (S-229: the session now writes
  a title change); an open tab was listed as archived too after a relaunch,
  and Shift-Cmd-T reopened the quit's window as a second copy (S-230); a real
  drag onto a folder's glyph in the rail landed after the folder, and dragging
  a row out of a folder could crash (S-232); a hidden address row left a line
  on Windows (S-231); the infobar container kept its height after its last bar
  went (S-227); the right-hand sidebar's test lost a real pointer on Windows
  (S-221); unit tests wrote into the person's data folder (S-220); the pin
  notice raced a queued move; a toast was lost on navigation; a toast of
  the whole window covered the infobars' text (S-244: it sits under them);
  turning dark pages on forgot a page's edge read it had never made, which
  the checks build stops on; with vertical tabs off, the tab strip gave its
  icons a transparent ground to measure their spinner's colour against.
- Tests that failed on Windows alone (S-242): pixel reads there need the
  browser test to ask for drawing; Windows labels Chromium's menu row with
  its mnemonic; a test of the quiet install now allows no browser sign-in,
  as a Stedding build; the elision test's Space name overflows Segoe UI too;
  the restore test's restarted mock client keeps addresses, as the first.

Why: each item is the owner's request of 2026-10-18 or a defect a user meets.
Removable when: never as a whole; the next rebase splits the features into
patches of their own and folds each fix into the patch that owns its code
(patches/README.md, the round-patch exception).
